<h3>1910</h3>

<p>In 1910, the city saw the completion of a new hospital, the completion of the initial portions of a city sewer system, a major extension of the drinking water mains and the opening of the city's first park. The Northern Pacific Railway also added hundreds of miles of track as its southern branch was completed into the communities of Flasher, Elgin, New Leipzig and Mott.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/350-1910-mar-8-fh-fire-cropped.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nineteen Teens 1910-1919 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The NP Railway east freighthouse, filled to capacity with flammable materials, was destroyed by fire on March 8. Despite efforts by Chief {% person "dave-taylor" %}Dave Taylor{% endperson %} and his volunteer firemen, the water pressure was inadequate to contain the fire with three lines. Losses were estimated at {% inflation 30000, 1910 %} for the building and its contents. A string of 12 boxcars alongside also caught fire, but were moved and extinguished. The lumber shed of the Bingenheimer Mercantile was also saved.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/282-1910-flood-at-mandan-homboe-rppc-mhsoc-jpg.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nineteen Teens 1910-1919 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The early breakup of the Missouri and Heart Rivers in March brought the highest watermark for flooding in the city since 1881, suspending mail and rail connections with the east. The 1910 spring flood prompted the NP Railway to build their new freight house and passenger depot on west Main St. (currently the Morton-Mandan Public Library). See <a href="/area-history/1910-spring-flood/">March 1910 Spring Flood</a> for more information.</p>

<p>The following summer, newspapers reported that packs of wild dogs "overrun the town ... seen out in the street opposite the Pioneer block ... is a disgraceful scene for travelers arriving by train at the Depot." Women in the city asked officials to require Heart River bathers to wear swimsuits because they were shocked by the "frank nudity" on display. White Star Livery, owned by E. McAuliff, acquired a new brougham, coupe, and cabriolet from the Twin Cities, but the additions could not reverse declining demand following the introduction of the automobile. A severe thunderstorm with large hail swept through the county on June 9, 1910. Hailstones were large enough to kill "good-sized" pigs, and crops and buildings were damaged throughout Morton County. Rainfall that evening measured 0.95 inches. The last serious attempt to subdivide Morton County occurred in October 1910. The <em>Glen Ullin News</em> promoted a north-south division with Glen Ullin as the new county seat. Flasher residents, supported by the <em>Flasher Hustler</em>, instead proposed an east-west division with Flasher as county seat.</p>

<h3>1911</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/274-st-henry-at-mandan.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nineteen Teens 1910-1919 page">
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/100-poster-june-1911-st-henry.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nineteen Teens 1910-1919 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The Missouri Slope Fair featured {% person "lucky-bob-st-henry" %}"Lucky Bob" St. Henry{% endperson %} with his Curtiss biplane "Sweetheart." Thousands attended the Fair to witness the demonstration, including passengers arriving on a special train from Bismarck.</p>

<p>A new waterworks pumping plant was installed and associated piping by the G. W. Haggart Co. of Fargo for a bid price of {% inflation 74141, 1911 %}. The year 1911 saw more creameries and more dairy cattle added to Morton County. Through the application of dry farming methods, hundreds of farmers harvested crops that paid well. The city of Mandan with a population near 4,000 continued to prosper.</p>

<h3>1912</h3>

<p>In March and April, city residents fought rising waters of the Missouri River, and later the Heart River. Water overflowed into Girard’s Addition. Minor flooding also occurred in the Syndicate residential district, with the entire fairgrounds under water. Upon vote of the City Commission, all downtown businesses were forced to connect to the sanitary sewer system when operation of outhouses were condemned.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/300-boyscouts-barr-cropped.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nineteen Teens 1910-1919 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On August 21, the first Boy Scout troop was established when the Episcopal Church sponsored Troop Number 1. {% person "william-cummins" %}William Cummins{% endperson %}, one of its members, achieved "First Class Scout" status on May 22, 1915, possibly the first member in the Missouri Valley Council to achieve the distinction. In October, {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %} would again return and speak to area residents with a "whistle-stop" at Mandan's Main Street depot during his campaign as the Progressive, or Bull Moose, Party presidential candidate. {% person "woodrow-wilson" %}Woodrow Wilson{% endperson %} would defeat both incumbent {% person "william-howard-taft" %}Howard Taft{% endperson %} and Roosevelt. See <a href="/area-history/1912-theodore-roosevelt-whistle-stop/">October 1912 Theodore Roosevelt Whistle Stop</a> for more information.</p>

<h3>1913</h3>

<p>In June, one of the most spectacular fires that ever occurred in Mandan completely destroyed the elevator of the Haight Lumber and Machinery Company, and caused a loss of {% inflation 8000, 1913 %}. Fire broke out about 2:00 PM after lightning struck the elevator's tower during a heavy thunderstorm. The stream of water from the firefighters’ hoses was unable to reach the top of the structure allowing the fire to work its way down, completely out of control. Within two hours, the building was in total ruins. According to Mr. Lanterman the owner, about 67% of the loss will be covered by insurance. The greater portion of the former Fort Abraham Lincoln and adjacent property for a total of four acres were deeded to the State of North Dakota in July 1913 and remains a state park today.</p>

<h3>1914</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/260-heart-river-dam-and-ladies.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nineteen Teens 1910-1919 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Efforts to dam the Heart River started as early as 1908, when the State Reform School (today's Youth Correctional Center) applied to use water to irrigate its land. With support from the Mandan Chautauqua Association and the Russell-Miller Milling Company, a dam was installed on the west side of the city. The 160-foot-long steel-and-concrete dam was larger than originally proposed and included a 90-foot-wide spillway.</p>

<p>The dam cost {% inflation 5671, 1914 %}. With $2,000 in state funding, the Mandan Commercial Club spearheaded efforts to raise the remaining money locally. The dam raised the river 7 feet and created a reservoir 2.5 to 3 miles long. It was completed just in time to control a record flow of nearly 6,500 cubic feet per second on June 27, following a cloudburst the previous day. The earlier record was approximately 6,100 cubic feet per second in June 1904.</p>

<h3>1915</h3>

<p>A proposal to organize western North Dakota and eastern Montana into a new state was advanced by Morton County Senator Martin, who claimed that eastern cities were "hogging the educational institutions." {% person "john-mushik" %}John Mushik{% endperson %} and {% person "a-s-brazada" %}A. S. Brazada{% endperson %} co-located their shoe shop and clothing-and-tailoring operation in the Williams Building, just east of {% person "l-n-cary" %}L. N. Cary's{% endperson %} office on West Main Street.</p>

<h3>1916</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/267-1909-car-ferry.jpg" alt="Missouri River automobile ferry in 1909">
    <figcaption>Missouri River automobile ferry, 1909</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The Mandan Automobile Club and the Bismarck Commercial Club purchased the river ferry operated by {% person "walter-gossard" %}Walter Gossard{% endperson %}. The city boosters paid Gossard $2,000 as a down payment on the gasoline ferry and began a campaign for the $1,300 balance. The Bismarck and Mandan clubs formed a stock corporation capitalized at $2,000. Daily income was in excess of $25 per day, with the expectation to repay the stock subscriptions by summer’s end.</p>

<p>The city's drinking water quality issues continued. Various citizens who declared they 'are willing to pay for water service, but not the mud they are currently getting!' Many citizens had to lug water from the remaining old water wells despite the installation of a "modern $80,000 waterworks system" in 1912. For example, Mr. T.J. Logan of the Golden West Laundry Co. installed a well at a cost of $1,300. The three month effort bored to a depth of 390 feet and with the aid of pumps is furnishing 1,000 gallons per hour of good water. The company had been temporarily closed since July after the city's muddy water prevented laundry from being washed clean. The artesian well was temporarily suspended in August 1916 to retrieve a tool down the well. The project was scheduled to be completed in 12 months, by May 1891. Total funds raised by sale of bonds for the project was $10,000. The Mandan Board of Health began enforcing the city's ordinance that all residents use sealed garbage cans to manage their trash.On November 28, Morton County was split into two. Grant County was established with its seat in Carson from the land in southwest half of the county.</p>

<h3>1917</h3>

<p>In January, on orders from the ND Supreme Court, Sheriff {% person "oscar-olson" %}Oscar Olson{% endperson %} smashed open 96 50-gallon wood barrels of claret stored in the basement of the {% place "collins-street-county-courthouse" %}Morton County Courthouse{% endplace %} for over a year and a half. The Supreme Court handed down the decision ordering the wine destroyed. The liquor was seized by {% person "william-wild-bill-langer" %}States Attorney Langer{% endperson %} from Hebron-area residents. Ownership of the wine was the focus of six different court proceedings before the highest court finalized the action.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1910s/334-np-park-color-postcard-1915-cropped.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Nineteen Teens 1910-1919 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In support of the war effort, the Northern Pacific Railroad decided to convert the 5-acre park, south of Main Street and adjacent to the depot into a vegetable garden. The park has been developed into one of the most beautiful parks along the route from St. Paul to the west coast. Once word got out, local businessmen were indignant. {% person "p-w-gillic" %}P. W. Gillic{% endperson %} offered alternate acreage to serve the purpose and preserve the park and its impact to the downtown area.</p>

<p>The Mandan City Commission considered a petition by the Mandan Commercial Club to construct a levee around the Syndicate and Dogtown, i.e., southwestern and southeastern parts of the city. According to the petition, property owners are requesting a dike be constructed from the Northern Pacific bridge at the place where it crosses the Heart River and what is known as First Crossing at the west end of the city, following along the north bank of the Heart River to the place where the south branch of the Northern Pacific railroad crosses the Heart River. (NOTE: The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers would design and install a series of levees in the 1950s to protect these areas of town.)</p>

<h3>1919</h3>

<p>In July, work started on the concrete foundation of the Western Auto Company building. The concrete was poured to a depth of 11 feet and two feet in thickness to expand to as many as five stories. The 100-by-140-foot garage was built on the southwest corner of Second Avenue NW and First Street NW, across from the Daily Pioneer office. The existing house on the lots was purchased from L. N. Cary by {% person "albert-lanterman" %}Albert Lanterman{% endperson %}, and it was moved onto the lot adjacent to the {% person "e-r-lanterman" %}E. R. Lanterman{% endperson %} residence on Third Avenue NW.</p>

<p>Some information presented on this page is based on research conducted by {% person "diane-boit" %}Diane Boit{% endperson %} on assignment to the <em>Mandan News</em> in her weekly "Those Were the Days" columns.</p>
